 },
 "limitations": "The machine was heavily shared while this ran (load average between about 15 and 90 on 12 cores, with a virtual machine using about seven cores), so single figures can move by tens of percent; the Go and Rust jobs of a request alternate within a round so they see the same load. Compare the ratios of Go to Rust wall time, the step figures and the peak memory rather than absolute seconds. The bundle was built from a5557db5e with only Markdown changes uncommitted, which its manifest records as dirty. Rust preparation's data row cache and schema lookups (8f8ac98b9) came after this run, so the Rust prepare step of the tank builds here is the slower one.",
 "findings": [
  "Every one of the 40 routed requests was prepared by Rust (`prepared_in_rust`), so a routed job no longer starts the Go exporter, and every routed job gave the same DPS as Go.",
  "A routed job's peak memory fell from the exporter's (median 93.0 MB for the Go job, about the same as the Go-prepared Rust job in the 2026-10-06 records) to 22.7 MB, since the job is one Rust process.",
  "The median Go to Rust job wall time ratio rose from 0.98 and 1.01 in the 2026-10-06 records to 1.15, and four jobs at a time ran 284 jobs a minute in Rust against 213 in Go, where the 2026-10-06 records had Rust below Go (316 to 390 and 349 to 371).",
  "Rust's prepare step takes 15 to 50 ms for casters, rogues, hunters and Balance druids against the exporter's 100 to 200 ms. The Warrior, Protection Paladin, Bear and Enhancement Shaman builds take 110 to 360 ms, as long as the exporter, because preparation builds hundreds of reset simulations for their stat aura combinations, as Go does."
 ],
